# Axiomancer Mobile - Project Rules

## Project Overview

This is the **React Native / Expo client** for the Axiomancer TTRPG. The
game rules, state shape, and randomness live in the
[`axiomancer-mechanics`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/axiomancer-mechanics)
engine — this repo is a presentation layer that consumes that package.

Treat the engine as the source of truth. If the UI needs to know
"how does combat work" or "what does an effect do", read the engine
package, do not invent rules in this repo.

## Core Concepts (delegated to the engine)

- **Heart / Body / Mind** stat triangle (Heart > Body > Mind > Heart).
- Combat is turn-based with stance + action + optional skill.
- Effects (buffs / debuffs) tick around a round in a specific phase
  order. The mobile UI **renders** effect state but does not **compute**
  it.
- Skills are themed around fallacies and paradoxes.

If any of the above feels wrong, check the engine — the engine wins.

## Code Conventions

### File Structure

- `app/<route>/<screen>.tsx` — Expo Router screen (UI shell only — props
  in, JSX out, no game-rule math).
- `app/<route>/<screen>.engine.ts` — pure presenter / selector logic
  for the screen. Maps engine state → UI view-model. **No JSX, no React
  hooks, no `Animated`.**
- `app/<route>/e2e/<feature>.engine.test.ts` — hermetic e2e test for
  the engine file above.
- `components/<Component>.tsx` — reusable, presentational, prop-driven.
- `components/<Component>.test.tsx` — render test (only when the
  component has branching UI logic worth pinning).
- `theme/axm.ts` — palette + font tokens; no logic.
- `assets/images/` — real assets (PNG / SVG) once the SVG placeholders
  are swapped per [`SVG_ASSET_SPEC.md`](./SVG_ASSET_SPEC.md).
- `specs/` — planning specs; one per body of work.
- `docs/` — design notes (testing standard, asset map, etc.).

### Naming Conventions

- camelCase for functions and variables.
- PascalCase for React components, types, and interfaces.
- SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE for module-level constants and palette tokens
  (e.g. `AXM.parchment`).
- Selector functions prefixed with verbs: `selectCombatViewModel`,
  `mapPlayerToHud`, `derive*`.

### Type System

- All exported functions must have explicit return types.
- View-model types live next to the presenter: `<screen>.engine.ts`
  exports `CombatViewModel`, etc.
- Discriminated unions for action / phase types.
- Prefer `Pick<T, K>` and `Partial<T>` over creating new interfaces
  when slicing engine types.

### Functional Patterns

- Presenters are pure functions: `(engineState, ...args) => viewModel`.
- Never mutate state directly — the engine's reducers are the only
  place state changes.
- Keep React state minimal: ephemeral UI state (selected stance preview,
  open modal) lives in `useState`; **persistent** state lives in the
  engine store.

### Mobile Design Philosophy

- Embrace the dark "Mörk Borg" tone — pitch black backgrounds, parchment
  highlights, blood / sulfur / rust accents only.
- Touch targets ≥ 44 × 44 dp.
- Every screen must be readable at 320 dp wide (smallest reasonable
  phone).
- Animations are decorative — every screen must be navigable with
  motion fully disabled (`reduceMotion`).
- Fonts: Pirata One (gothic display), IM Fell English (serif body),
  Bebas Neue (sans labels), JetBrains Mono (numerics).

## Hermetic E2E Testing (REQUIRED for every implementation)

Full standard: `docs/testing.md`. Reference: the first hermetic e2e
under `app/<route>/e2e/` (delivered by Spec 01).

Every implementation lands with at least one hermetic e2e test that
drives the change through the highest-level public entry point of its
module.

**Hermetic** = all three:

- **Self-contained:** no network, no real `AsyncStorage`, no real
  filesystem, no real font loader, no real `Animated` driver, no real
  timers (use `jest.useFakeTimers()` when timers matter).
- **Deterministic:** `Math.random` stubbed (or seeded RNG via the
  engine's `Rng` abstraction once Spec 11 in the engine ships). No
  wall-clock, no env vars, no PIDs.
- **Isolated:** `afterEach(() => jest.restoreAllMocks())`. No shared
  mutable state across tests. Fixtures deep-cloned by the code under
  test, not by the test.

**E2E** = drives the change through the public entry point of the
module — the screen's presenter (`select<Screen>ViewModel`) for UI
work; the engine store's lifecycle (`createGameStore(asyncStorageAdapter,
…)` driven through actions) for state work — *not* through a private
helper.

### Conventions

- **Location:** `app/<route>/e2e/<feature>.engine.test.ts` for hermetic
  full-flow tests. Companion engine code at
  `app/<route>/e2e/<feature>.engine.ts`. Pure unit tests stay alongside
  the source.
- **Required suites:** happy path, every terminal / branch the change
  can reach, invariants (view-model fields are total — no `undefined`
  where the UI expects a string), and at least one
  `createGameStore(memoryAdapter, …)` lifecycle assertion that
  `jest.spyOn(memoryAdapter, 'save')` was *not* called when the change
  doesn't write.
- **Screen changes** must delegate to a presenter function with its own
  hermetic e2e test. Inline math / inline data shaping in `*.tsx`
  blocks this — extract it.

Before declaring done: `npm test` green twice + `npx tsc --noEmit`
clean.

## Things to NOT do

- Do not import `axiomancer-mechanics` Node-only adapters from a screen
  (filesystem persistence is not bundleable on RN). Use the in-memory
  adapter for tests; `AsyncStorage` adapter (Spec 09) for runtime.
- Do not write game rules in this repo. If a rule is missing, add it to
  the engine and re-version the dependency.
- Do not add new `Math.random()` call sites in screens — go through the
  engine's RNG.
- Do not bypass the spec workflow for medium / large changes. Small
  fixes are fine; anything touching multiple screens or the data model
  warrants a spec.
